package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * The pull-protocol base for everything iteration-related in the
 * stdlib (Collections.md § Stream). Implementers override `next()`
 * to yield elements; combinator methods on Stream itself consume
 * `next()` and inherit to every subclass.
 *
 * ## v1 minimal surface
 *
 * Just the pull primitive plus `count()` and `forEach((T) -> void)`
 * terminals. The intermediate-combinator wrappers (MapStream,
 * FilterStream, TakeStream, ...) and the rest of the terminal set
 * (fold, reduce, anyMatch, ..., collect) land in P6.5+.
 *
 * ## "Abstract" via empty-default convention
 *
 * Cajeta doesn't have abstract-method enforcement at vtable build
 * today, so Stream's `next()` lands as a concrete method returning
 * `None` by default. Implementers override; the default is never
 * meaningfully invoked because user code only calls `next()` on
 * subclasses. (Future work: real abstract-method support so
 * forgetting to override is a compile-time error.)
 *
 * ## Stream vs Optional integration
 *
 * Per Collections.md, Optional<T> extends Stream<T> with single-
 * shot next(). That lands once method-override on generic stdlib
 * classes is verified end-to-end via ArrayStream below.
 */
public class Stream<T> {
    /**
     * Pull the next element. Implementers override; default returns
     * an empty Optional.
     */
    public Optional<T> next() {
        return heap Optional<T>(false, null);
    }

    /**
     * Count elements by walking next() to exhaustion. The simplest
     * terminal; no lambda needed.
     */
    public int32 count() {
        int32 n = 0;
        Optional<T> o = this.next();
        while (o.isPresent()) {
            n = n + 1;
            o = this.next();
        }
        return n;
    }
}
